 ## Proof
 
-1.1 Omit zero weights. Positivity of $\bar\rho-p_i\rho_i$ implies $\ker\bar\rho\subseteq\ker\rho_i$ (take its quadratic form on a kernel vector), so every relative entropy below is finite. Expanding the spectral cross traces gives $$S(\bar\rho)-\sum_i p_iS(\rho_i)=\sum_i p_iD(\rho_i\Vert\bar\rho)\ge0$$ by the finite-state QRE nonnegativity supplier. [F1, F2, F3, F4, given, algebra, construct]
+1.1 Omit zero weights. For each $i$ with $p_i>0$, $\bar\rho-p_i\rho_i=\sum_{j\ne i}p_j\rho_j$ is non-negative. If $v\in\ker\bar\rho$, its quadratic form under this sum is zero; every summand is nonnegative, so $\langle\rho_i v,v\rangle=0$ and [F5] gives $\rho_i v=0$. Hence $\operatorname{supp}\rho_i\subseteq\operatorname{supp}\bar\rho$ and every $D(\rho_i\|\bar\rho)$ is finite. The trace formula [F6], linearity of trace [F4], and $\bar\rho=\sum_i p_i\rho_i$ give $$\sum_i p_iD(\rho_i\Vert\bar\rho)=S(\bar\rho)-\sum_i p_iS(\rho_i)\ge0$$ by [F2]. [F1, F2, F4, F5, F6, given, algebra, construct]
 
-2.1 For the upper bound, diagonalize each $\rho_i=\sum_j\lambda_{ij}|v_{ij}\rangle\langle v_{ij}|$, omitting zero eigenvalues, and purify the mixture as $|v\rangle=\sum_{ij}\sqrt{p_i\lambda_{ij}}|v_{ij}\rangle\otimes|i,j\rangle$. Its label marginal $G$ has diagonal $q_{ij}=p_i\lambda_{ij}$ and has the same nonzero eigenvalues as $\bar\rho$ by Schmidt decomposition. [F1, F2, F3, F4, given, algebra, construct, step 1.1]
+2.1 For the upper bound, diagonalize each $\rho_i=\sum_j\lambda_{ij}|v_{ij}\rangle\langle v_{ij}|$, omitting zero weights and eigenvalues, and purify the mixture as $|v\rangle=\sum_{ij}\sqrt{p_i\lambda_{ij}}|v_{ij}\rangle\otimes|i,j\rangle$. Its label marginal $G$ has diagonal $q_{ij}=p_i\lambda_{ij}$ and has the same nonzero eigenvalues as $\bar\rho$ by Schmidt decomposition. The logarithm product law in [F6] gives $H(q)=H(p)+\sum_i p_iS(\rho_i)$. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F6, given, algebra, construct, step 1.1]
 
-3.1 For $\Delta=\operatorname{diag}(q)$, positivity gives $\operatorname{supp}G\subseteq\operatorname{supp}\Delta$; hence $$0\le D(G\Vert\Delta)=H(q)-S(G)=H(p)+\sum_i p_iS(\rho_i)-S(\bar\rho).$$ This proves both bounds, including singleton/zero-weight cases. [F1, F2, F3, F4, given, algebra, construct, step 2.1] ∎
+3.1 For $\Delta=\operatorname{diag}(q)$, if $v\in\ker\Delta$, then $\langle Gv,v\rangle=0$ because $G$ is non-negative and has diagonal $q$; [F5] gives $Gv=0$. Thus $\operatorname{supp}G\subseteq\operatorname{supp}\Delta$. The formula [F6] now gives $$0\le D(G\Vert\Delta)=H(q)-S(G)=H(p)+\sum_i p_iS(\rho_i)-S(\bar\rho).$$ This proves both bounds, including singleton/zero-weight cases. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, given, algebra, construct, step 2.1] ∎
